# ProxyPilot Environment Configuration — canonical key list
#
# This file is the source of truth for which environment variables
# ProxyPilot expects. install.sh generates a populated .env from this
# template; update.sh compares against it on every upgrade and appends
# any newly-introduced keys with a `# TODO` placeholder so existing
# installs don't silently start without required configuration.
#
# Never commit a real .env file. Real secrets live only in /opt/proxypilot/.env
# (or wherever the install was placed) with mode 0600.

# Server Configuration
PORT=3001
NODE_ENV=production
DOMAIN=admin.example.com

# Authentication — generate via: openssl rand -base64 64
JWT_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_64_BYTES_RANDOM
SESSION_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_64_BYTES_RANDOM

# DB-at-rest encryption key for TOTP secrets and other sensitive
# columns. 32 bytes hex. Generate via: openssl rand -hex 32
# Losing this key means existing TOTP secrets cannot be decrypted —
# every user will need to re-enroll their authenticator.
TOTP_ENCRYPTION_KEY=CHANGE_ME_64_HEX_CHARS

# Admin User (consumed only on first boot to seed the user row)
ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
ADMIN_PASSWORD=
ADMIN_TOTP_SECRET=

# Database — lives in a dedicated subdirectory so the surrounding
# `data/` directory can stay world-traversable for Caddy. The DB
# directory itself is locked to 0700 by db.js on every boot; the DB
# file to 0600. Pre-update SQLite backups are placed alongside it.
DATABASE_PATH=/data/db/proxypilot.db

# Account lockout (consumed by routes/auth.js)
# - MAX_FAILURES: lock the account after this many failed attempts
# - FAILURE_WINDOW_MIN: failures outside this window restart the
#   streak counter (so a slow drip of typos doesn't accumulate)
# - LOCKOUT_MIN: how long the lock holds before the user can retry
LOGIN_MAX_FAILURES=10
LOGIN_FAILURE_WINDOW_MIN=30
LOGIN_LOCKOUT_MIN=30

# Session lifetime (consumed by middleware/auth.js)
# - TTL_HOURS: absolute upper bound on a single login (re-login
#   required past this regardless of activity)
# - IDLE_HOURS: sliding inactivity window. Any authenticated request
#   resets it; idle past this many hours = session revoked.
# - RETENTION_DAYS: how long revoked / expired session rows stick
#   around for forensics before sweepStaleSessions() reaps them.
SESSION_TTL_HOURS=24
SESSION_IDLE_HOURS=4
SESSION_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# Streaming-terminal limits (consumed by routes/terminal-ws.js)
# - MAX_SESSIONS: refuse a 4th concurrent terminal from the same user
# - IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS: kill any session idle for >15 min
# - OUTPUT_BACKPRESSURE_BYTES: pause the PTY when ws.bufferedAmount
#   crosses this high-water mark, so a runaway producer (`yes`,
#   `find /`) can't self-DoS the browser tab.
TERMINAL_MAX_SESSIONS=3
TERMINAL_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS=900000
TERMINAL_OUTPUT_BACKPRESSURE_BYTES=1000000

# Caddy Configuration Path
CADDY_SITES_DIR=/etc/caddy/sites
CADDY_CONFIG_FILE=/etc/caddy/Caddyfile
# Operator-owned directory for hand-written Caddy snippets that
# ProxyPilot must never rewrite. Imported from the main Caddyfile.
CADDY_CUSTOM_DIR=/etc/caddy/custom
ACME_EMAIL=

# Global TLS mode: 'acme' (default — Caddy issues Let's Encrypt certs via ACME)
# or 'manual' (serve admin-pasted certificates and never attempt ACME for
# covered domains — for networks that BLOCK Let's Encrypt/ACME endpoints).
# Seeded into app_settings on first boot; the TLS Certificates admin page
# toggles it at runtime. Manual-cert files live in CADDY_MANUAL_CERT_DIR
# (default /etc/caddy/pp-manual-certs, key 0600 caddy). Expiry-warning window:
# PROXYPILOT_CERT_EXPIRY_WARN_DAYS (default 21); check schedule:
# PROXYPILOT_CERT_EXPIRY_CRON (default '15 3 * * *').
TLS_MODE=acme

# Install-time certificate seeding (manual TLS). install.sh can stage a
# PEM cert+key (e.g. a Cloudflare Origin CA cert) into this directory; on
# first boot the backend imports it into the SAME tls_certificates store the
# TLS Certificates admin page manages, then deletes the staged private key.
# Default is /data/seed-tls (the mounted data dir inside the container).
# PROXYPILOT_SEED_TLS_PASSPHRASE overrides the passphrase from the staged
# meta.json for an encrypted key; normally leave both unset.
# PROXYPILOT_SEED_TLS_DIR=/data/seed-tls
# PROXYPILOT_SEED_TLS_PASSPHRASE=

# Phase B host-side agent: route Caddy adapt + reload through
# proxypilot-agent (true) or the legacy nsenter execOnHost path
# (false). Phase B is dual-tracked: the new path is wired but
# disabled by default. Phase F flips this to `true` after burn-in
# on production. Leave it at `false` until then.
PROXYPILOT_USE_AGENT_FOR_CADDY=false

# ── Mock2 (dev/build module) — absence-by-installation (ADR-001) ──
# Mock2 is the reverse-trust dev-plane module (project containers the
# orchestrator writes into directly). It is OFF by default and MUST be
# absent on production hosts. Three layers gate it, checked at boot:
#   1. Pin file /etc/proxypilot/mock2.production.pin — if present, Mock2 is
#      hard-off no matter what this flag says (a warning is logged). The
#      operator creates the pin; ProxyPilot never writes it.
#   2. This flag. When false: no /api/mock2 route, no data/db/mock2.db file,
#      no model-credential store, no nav entry — a disabled host behaves
#      byte-for-byte like one that never shipped the module.
#   3. Enabling also requires at least one model connector before the runner
#      will do work (env var AND presence of credentials).
# A fresh install.sh now enables Mock2 by default (still prompts; the pin file
# forces it off). update.sh appends this key as `false` to existing hosts and
# never touches an existing value, so upgrades never SILENTLY enable it — an
# operator opts an upgrading host in explicitly with `update.sh --enable-mock2`.
MOCK2_ENABLED=false

# Runtime data root for Mock2 (per-project bare git repos, framework seed,
# per-domain certs — populated by later phases). Created only when Mock2 is
# enabled. Note: the Mock2 SQLite state file itself lives next to the main
# DB at data/db/mock2.db, not here.
MOCK2_DATA_DIR=/var/lib/proxypilot/mock2

# Directory for Mock2-owned Caddy site files (Phase M1). Mock2 writes one file
# per parent domain here and adds a single `import <dir>/*.caddy` line to the
# main Caddyfile — it never touches the per-service /etc/caddy/sites tree or the
# operator's /etc/caddy/custom tree. Only written on an enabled host.
MOCK2_CADDY_DIR=/etc/caddy/mock2

# This host's public IP(s), comma-separated (Phase M1). Used to cross-check that
# a parent domain's wildcard DNS actually points here before requesting a probe
# certificate. Leave blank to skip the cross-check (the wildcard still has to
# resolve to *something*, it just isn't compared to this host's address). Also
# cross-checks a project's custom-domain A record (Phase M2).
MOCK2_PUBLIC_IP=

# Base Incus image for Mock2 project containers (Phase M2). Each project is
# launched unprivileged as m2-<id> on its own per-project bridge (Phase M4) from
# this image. Only used on an enabled host; override for hosts that mirror
# images elsewhere.
MOCK2_BASE_IMAGE=images:debian/12

# Filtering egress proxy port (Phase M4, ADR-010). Each project container is
# fenced to a default-deny bridge and reaches the internet ONLY through the
# host's squid proxy on this port (HTTP(S)_PROXY is baked into the container
# env). squid is installed only when Mock2 is enabled (scripts/mock2-enable-
# egress.sh, run by install.sh on enable). If you change this, re-run that
# script so squid's http_port matches. Default is squid's standard 3128.
MOCK2_EGRESS_PROXY_PORT=3128

# Egress policy mode (Phase M4, ADR-010). 'allowlist' (default) restricts each
# project container to its per-project allowlisted hosts. 'allow-all' is MONITOR
# mode: every project bridge may reach any host, but egress is still funneled
# through squid so its access log records every destination — use it to discover
# what to allow, then switch back to enforce. This is the default when no admin
# has set the mode in the UI (Project → Egress); the stored setting overrides it.
MOCK2_EGRESS_MODE=allowlist

# Integration-gate mode (the block/approve loop relief valve). Controls what the
# integration-truthfulness gate does with a build it would block at finish:
#   'enforce' (default) — block until an admin resolves it (the safe posture).
#   'pending'           — deploy the build instead and record the outstanding
#                         LIVE checks as pending-operator-verification, so you can
#                         test the running app; the honesty check moves to a human
#                         live check rather than being dropped.
#   'monitor'           — record the findings but never block or pend; the build
#                         completes so you can see it run. Loosest.
# This is the default when no admin has set the mode in the UI (Admin queue →
# Integration gate mode); the stored setting overrides it.
MOCK2_INTEGRATION_GATE_MODE=enforce

# Path to squid's access log on the host, read (best-effort, read-only) to show
# a project's egress traffic in the UI (Project → Egress → Traffic log). Default
# is Debian's standard location.
MOCK2_EGRESS_ACCESS_LOG=/var/log/squid/access.log

# LEGACY global default for which agent harness drives a mock2 build cycle. The
# harness is now selectable PER PROJECT (Project → Details → Build harness:
# "ProxyPilot" | "Claude"); an explicit per-project choice always wins over this
# flag. This flag only sets what a project with NO explicit choice runs:
# unset/empty (the default) uses the hand-rolled, provider-agnostic ProxyPilot
# harness — unchanged behavior; "sdk" uses the Claude harness (the Claude Agent
# SDK, @anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk — docs/agent-sdk-migration.md). The SDK is
# a standard backend dependency (admin/backend/package.json; the committed
# admin/backend/.npmrc resolves its zod@^4 peer vs the backend's zod@^3 with
# legacy-peer-deps), so a normal `npm install` / update.sh run installs it.
# Fully reversible; while no project selects Claude and this flag is unset, the
# SDK is never imported.
BUILD_RUNNER=

# Anthropic API key for the Claude harness — a pay-as-you-go platform.claude.com
# API key, NEVER a claude.ai subscription login. Optional: the Claude harness
# first uses the build_runner slot's Anthropic connector key (encrypted in the
# DB); this env var is the fallback when that slot isn't pointed at Anthropic.
# Read SERVER-SIDE only — it is never sent to the browser and never enters a
# project container (the UI only ever sees a "configured" boolean). Never log it.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# OpenAI (and every other non-Anthropic provider: Gemini, Ollama,
# openai_compatible) is configured as a MODEL CONNECTOR in the admin UI, NOT via
# an env var: the API key is stored encrypted in the DB (TOTP_ENCRYPTION_KEY) and
# read server-side only, exactly like the primary Anthropic-connector path above.
# There is deliberately no OPENAI_API_KEY env var — the ANTHROPIC_API_KEY fallback
# exists only because the Claude Agent SDK harness (BUILD_RUNNER) reads its key
# from the process environment; the neutral model client (openai/gemini/ollama)
# always takes its key from the connector row. Create the OpenAI connector, pick a
# model (e.g. gpt-5.6-terra), and assign it to a slot. See docs/features/model-routing.md.

# Model the Claude harness uses when the key comes from ANTHROPIC_API_KEY and
# the build_runner slot is NOT an Anthropic connector (an Anthropic slot's model
# is always used as-is). Empty = the pinned default (claude-opus-4-8).
CLAUDE_HARNESS_MODEL=

# Web search for the build chat's ASK lane (questions about the codebase /
# bounded read-and-run tasks). Uses the Claude API's SERVER-SIDE web_search tool:
# the search runs inside Anthropic's infrastructure during the model call, so
# the project fence stays sealed and no extra credentials are needed. Anthropic
# connectors only (other providers never get the tool). Default ON for the ask
# lane; set 'off' to disable. Billed by Anthropic per search on top of tokens.
MOCK2_WEB_SEARCH=on

# Same, for the BUILD runner (the audited change cycles). Default OFF — builds
# stay deterministic unless the operator opts in. Set 'on' to let build cycles
# look up e.g. API documentation mid-build (Anthropic connectors only).
MOCK2_RUNNER_WEB_SEARCH=off

# Model ROUTING for build cycles (migration 525). The define audit classifies
# each task (kind + difficulty 1-5); an admin-editable knowledge base
# (Settings → Model routing) maps task kind → model override / escalation
# model / effort; a failed/halted attempt of the same request escalates the
# next attempt to the stronger model. Every decision is stamped on the cycle
# and its outcome recorded, so the dictionary is tuned from evidence.
#   on     — decide AND apply (default)
#   shadow — decide + record, but keep the slot model/settings (trial mode)
#   off    — skip entirely (pre-routing behavior)
MOCK2_ROUTING=on

# Global fallback escalation model, used when a rule has no escalate_model of
# its own. Must be a model id the build_runner slot's connector can serve
# (e.g. claude-opus-4-8). Empty (default) = no escalation unless a rule sets one.
MOCK2_ESCALATE_MODEL=

# Per-phase model routing (phase-routing@1): EVERY build (full / mvp / quick)
# runs as five phases (recon / plan / implement-3a/3b / summarize / review),
# each on its own model tier, resolved at cycle start from which providers
# (Anthropic/OpenAI) hold a usable connector credential; the build
# conversation runs on the map's implement model. The resolved map is
# recorded in the change record. The dashboard's Admin queue → "Per-phase
# model routing" card stores a setting that wins over this env var.
#   on  — resolve + apply the per-phase map (default)
#   off — the single-model path everywhere
MOCK2_PHASE_ROUTING=on

# Cost posture over the resolved per-phase map (five presets; the dashboard
# card's "Cost posture" select stores a setting that wins over this env var).
# Provider-aware — each preset resolves against the providers that actually
# hold a usable credential:
#   default     — the manually set configuration, as resolved (ships default)
#   suggested   — the recommended tier model at every phase
#   ultra_cheap — cheapest available model everywhere (Luna, else Haiku)
#   balanced    — the mid tier everywhere (Terra / Sonnet level)
#   max_quality — best available flagship everywhere (Fable 5, else Sol)
MOCK2_PHASE_POSTURE=default

# ---- stall watchdog (stuck-build recovery) ----
# Streaming model calls abort when NO bytes arrive for this long (a silently
# dropped API connection otherwise waits out the full output-scaled deadline —
# ~106 minutes at a 128k-token budget). Milliseconds; default 300000 (5 min);
# floored at 30000; "off" or 0 disables.
MOCK2_MODEL_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS=300000

# Silence windows for a RUNNING build (cycle-event activity), in minutes. Both
# are FALLBACKS — the dashboard's Admin queue → "Stall watchdog" card stores
# settings that win over these. RESTART (default 10): the chat offers the
# one-click "Restart build". STALL (default 30): the 60s sweep hard-stops the
# build on its own (interrupted + lock released + a chat note; it resumes from
# the last checkpoint). The hard stop is kept generous on purpose — a single
# long model turn writes no events while it thinks — and never fires before
# the Restart offer. Floored at 2, capped at 1440.
MOCK2_RESTART_STALL_MINUTES=10
MOCK2_STALL_MINUTES=30

# No-progress circuit breaker threshold (harness safety, both runners). A build cycle
# that makes no progress for this many consecutive turns — no tool call, near-identical
# repeated responses, or the same action with no state change — is auto-halted as
# BLOCKED (needs attention, resumable) instead of re-prompting forever. Small single
# digit; default 3, clamped to >=2. See docs/agent-sdk-migration.md.
BUILD_NO_PROGRESS_LIMIT=3

# ---- e2e/journey smoke gate connectors (relevance-gated escalation) ----
# After a build deploys, a smoke gate runs the cheap HTTP/API journey check ALWAYS.
# Two heavier connectors escalate ONLY when the change's diff/metadata warrants them
# (browser: user-facing render/flow paths → rendered-DOM interaction checks from the
# project's state/ui-checks.json; db: data/state paths → migrations applied to a COPY
# of the database + a boot check) — never on every cycle. Both are ON by default
# (a UI regression once shipped through five green gates because nothing exercised
# the rendered DOM). Turning a connector OFF (SMOKE_BROWSER_ENABLED=0 /
# SMOKE_DB_ENABLED=0) is a deliberate choice to test the deployed build live by
# hand: the cycle SHIPS (logged as 'disabled'), it does NOT fail. What DOES fail the
# cycle is a connector left ON that a change warrants but that then cannot RUN
# (playwright missing / no DSN) — a warranted-but-unrunnable check must never read as
# success. The browser connector needs `playwright` installed in admin/backend
# (npm install playwright) + a Chromium. Every run/skip + reason is logged on the
# cycle. See docs/agent-sdk-migration.md.
SMOKE_BROWSER_ENABLED=true       # drive the deployed UI via Playwright on a browser trigger (0 = ship for live human testing)
SMOKE_DB_ENABLED=true            # migration dry-run on a DB copy + boot check on a data trigger (0 = ship for live human testing)
SMOKE_GATE_ENFORCING=false       # true → a failing always-on HTTP layer fails the cycle
SMOKE_REQUIRE_TRIGGERED=true     # an ENABLED connector that a change warrants but that cannot run fails the cycle
# Optional: point the browser connector at a system/pre-provisioned Chromium
# instead of the revision the installed playwright package would download:
# SMOKE_BROWSER_EXECUTABLE=/usr/bin/chromium
# Optional comma-separated glob overrides (defaults cover public/**, *.html/css/jsx,
# migrations/**, *.sql, schema/seed/bootstrap, etc.):
# SMOKE_BROWSER_GLOBS=public/**,**/*.html,**/*.css
# SMOKE_DB_GLOBS=migrations/**,**/*.sql,**/schema/**

# ---- Domain provisioning ("Add Domain" self-service page) ----
# OPTIONAL fallback for the global Cloudflare API token — the normal way
# to connect Cloudflare is the dashboard (Domains -> Cloudflare
# connection), which stores the token encrypted and takes precedence
# over this env var. Scopes required: Zone -> DNS -> Edit and
# Zone -> Zone -> Read, limited to the relevant zone(s). Used by DEFAULT
# for every DNS-01 domain (wildcards, geo-blocked domains, the DNS-01
# list); a per-domain token on the Add Domain form overrides it.
# Standard Let's Encrypt (HTTP-01) domains never touch this.
# The DNS-01 method also needs Caddy's Cloudflare plugin — install it
# from the Domains page (one click; update.sh re-installs it after
# caddy package upgrades) or by hand:
#   caddy add-package github.com/caddy-dns/cloudflare
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=

# ---- Web Push (VAPID, RFC 8292) ----
# Generated automatically by install.sh, and backfilled by update.sh on an
# existing install that predates this feature. You should not need to touch
# these.
#
# WHAT THEY DO: the PUBLIC key is handed to the browser when it subscribes to
# push, and the resulting subscription only accepts pushes signed by the
# matching PRIVATE key. That is how a push service (Google's FCM for
# Chrome/Edge/Android, Mozilla for Firefox, Apple for Safari/iOS) knows a
# notification really came from this server.
#
# THEY ARE A PAIR. Replacing either one invalidates every existing browser
# subscription and every device has to re-subscribe. Back them up with the
# database. They do NOT encrypt the notification: payload encryption uses keys
# the browser generates per subscription, so these keys alone decrypt nothing.
#
# VAPID_SUBJECT is how a push service contacts you before it starts rejecting
# traffic — a mailto: or https: URL. Leave it pointing somewhere real.
#
# These are listed UNCOMMENTED (with empty values) on purpose: update.sh's
# env-sync only sees uncommented keys, and a commented entry here would mean an
# existing install never gets them backfilled.
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:admin@example.com

# ---- Zip uploads (static-site bundles + LXC app drops) ----
# All optional; the defaults suit a "here is my built dist/ folder"
# workflow. Uploaded archives are staged on disk while the UI's
# conflict-confirmation round-trip is in flight, then extracted
# server-side (static sites) or streamed into the container as a tar
# (LXC). Caps guard against zip bombs.
# PROXYPILOT_ZIP_UPLOAD_LIMIT_BYTES=268435456   # max .zip size (256 MB)
# PROXYPILOT_ZIP_EXTRACT_LIMIT_BYTES=1073741824 # max extracted size (1 GB)
# PROXYPILOT_ZIP_MAX_ENTRIES=20000              # max entries per archive
# PROXYPILOT_ZIP_STAGE_TTL_MS=1800000           # staged-upload expiry (30 min)
# ZIP_UPLOAD_TMP_DIR=/tmp/proxypilot-zip-uploads
# PROXYPILOT_STARTUP_RUN_TIMEOUT_MS=120000      # LXC startup-script run cap

# ---- Mock2 pre-build guardrails ----
# The Define-stage block: a project that has built before but has zero
# confirmed rules is refused with "run Define first" (the rule-coverage gate
# has nothing behavioural to check until it has rules). The refusal always
# carries an escape hatch — press Build again within the hour — but this
# switches the pre-build block off entirely for installs that drive Define on
# their own schedule. The rule-coverage GATE still runs and still reports.
# MOCK2_RULES_GATE=off
# The symptom-chase cap: the third attempt at substantially the same symptom is
# diagnosed instead of built. Same shape, same escape hatch.
# MOCK2_SYMPTOM_CAP=off
